1
00:00:20,884 --> 00:00:29,651
[SPEAKER_00]: I was reading that sutta of Arahant Talaputta.

2
00:00:30,772 --> 00:00:31,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you remember it?

3
00:00:31,773 --> 00:00:32,174
[SPEAKER_00]: With the...

4
00:00:33,335 --> 00:00:36,417
[SPEAKER_01]: He was the... that comedian... comedian actor.

5
00:00:38,199 --> 00:00:42,022
[SPEAKER_00]: That one with the... he's Arahant in his... that poem and he's saying

6
00:00:45,077 --> 00:00:46,438
[SPEAKER_00]: How he wanted to live.

7
00:00:48,019 --> 00:00:49,341
[SPEAKER_00]: When will I live in the forest?

8
00:00:49,361 --> 00:00:54,385
[SPEAKER_00]: He's fantasizing, romanticizing about the mossy, mossy rocks.

9
00:00:54,425 --> 00:00:55,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, when will I hear the peacock's cry?

10
00:00:55,766 --> 00:00:59,269
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when he was there, the mind wanted to go back to lay life.

11
00:00:59,289 --> 00:00:59,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

12
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:03,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he says, now he won't.

13
00:01:03,272 --> 00:01:07,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he starts to say, when will I be a great monk?

14
00:01:07,836 --> 00:01:08,877
[SPEAKER_00]: When will I be at peace?

15
00:01:10,547 --> 00:01:24,721
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he starts to tell off his mind, ask the mind questions like, why didn't you urge me on before to become an ascetic, to go to the forest, and yet now, you want me to go after the things that you said I should be giving up.

16
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:28,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I've given them up.

17
00:01:29,181 --> 00:01:33,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm not going to go and do those things which I vomited up.

18
00:01:34,063 --> 00:01:35,163
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not going to eat it again.

19
00:01:35,643 --> 00:01:40,465
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he continues to berate the mind in such a way, like...

20
00:01:42,322 --> 00:01:46,224
[SPEAKER_00]: And at the end, it starts to say, well, now I'm in control.

21
00:01:46,344 --> 00:01:50,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm going to tie you down until you sort of get tamed, yeah.

22
00:01:50,825 --> 00:01:56,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's... that's... that is everyone's relationship with their own mind, usually... well, not usually, always.

23
00:01:56,528 --> 00:02:02,910
[SPEAKER_01]: The only difference is how much perspective in regard to it you have, or how much you identify with it.

24
00:02:03,670 --> 00:02:10,253
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind presents you these ideas, and then you accept them, and then you act out of them, and then you kind of assume the ownership over them.

25
00:02:11,777 --> 00:02:16,919
[SPEAKER_01]: But you didn't really have say in their creation, in their arising.

26
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:20,201
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind came up with it.

27
00:02:21,121 --> 00:02:22,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, oh yeah, I had these thoughts.

28
00:02:22,442 --> 00:02:29,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you realize, also, it's not about me coming up with it, or me owning or disowning that which is not in my control.

29
00:02:30,385 --> 00:02:35,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Up to me is basically whether I choose to accept it, what the mind presented me with.

30
00:02:35,707 --> 00:02:38,588
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, whether you choose to accept what the eyes want to see.

31
00:02:38,668 --> 00:02:39,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Eyes are pulling you in a direction,

32
00:02:40,589 --> 00:02:41,850
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't have to accept it.

33
00:02:42,590 --> 00:02:43,791
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to go with it.

34
00:02:45,052 --> 00:02:51,437
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if people have no self-awareness, no perspective developed through sense-restraint, for them, that pull is theirs.

35
00:02:51,877 --> 00:02:52,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Ironically.

36
00:02:52,558 --> 00:02:57,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, they're being pulled against their will, but they believe that's what they want to do.

37
00:02:58,743 --> 00:03:07,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, Talaputta, and that's what already had... had already some wisdom there, because he already was able to see a mind as this thing.

38
00:03:08,269 --> 00:03:12,472
[SPEAKER_01]: That, oh, yeah, you urged me to do this, I did this, now I want to go back.

39
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:15,493
[SPEAKER_01]: If he didn't have wisdom, he would have just thought, I want to go back.

40
00:03:17,094 --> 00:03:19,616
[SPEAKER_01]: But he questioned, well, is this what I want?

41
00:03:19,716 --> 00:03:24,379
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what my mind wants, it's clear enough, but is this what I want?

42
00:03:26,818 --> 00:03:28,340
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't want to eat what I vomited.

43
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:30,221
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was miserable over there.

44
00:03:30,241 --> 00:03:32,944
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I left, to try this.

45
00:03:33,004 --> 00:03:34,745
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't want to do it.

46
00:03:35,166 --> 00:03:36,167
[SPEAKER_01]: But the mind still wants it.

47
00:03:36,287 --> 00:03:37,167
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's a conflict.

48
00:03:37,348 --> 00:03:37,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a choice.

49
00:03:38,148 --> 00:03:44,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Give in to the mind, in order to not endure the conflict, or tame the mind, in order to resolve the conflict.

50
00:03:44,874 --> 00:03:47,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there will be no more conflict when you are in charge.

51
00:03:48,690 --> 00:03:54,214
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the... the mind offered him something good in the beginning.

52
00:03:54,274 --> 00:03:55,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly, yeah, yeah, yeah.

53
00:03:55,855 --> 00:04:02,040
[SPEAKER_01]: He was... And obviously, that was due to, you know, his own pondering, reflection, choices.

54
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:04,081
[SPEAKER_01]: He did meet the Buddha as well.

55
00:04:04,461 --> 00:04:07,924
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then he thought, actually, even his own mind thought, this is worthwhile doing.

56
00:04:07,964 --> 00:04:10,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even his own mind was not happy in that environment.

57
00:04:11,947 --> 00:04:17,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why I presented him with these romantic ideas of the forest and the jungle.

58
00:04:18,091 --> 00:04:24,735
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when the, you know, the reality of those romantic ideas sets in, the hardship, enduring the elements of the insects, then the mind changes itself.

59
00:04:25,035 --> 00:04:26,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, actually, I want to...

60
00:04:26,656 --> 00:04:27,296
[SPEAKER_01]: I changed my mind.

61
00:04:28,057 --> 00:04:29,918
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go back.

62
00:04:29,958 --> 00:04:30,858
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind has changed.

63
00:04:31,059 --> 00:04:33,860
[SPEAKER_01]: He wants to go back, but shall I follow it?

64
00:04:34,321 --> 00:04:34,461
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

65
00:04:35,181 --> 00:04:35,982
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like what we're saying.

66
00:04:36,442 --> 00:04:39,624
[SPEAKER_01]: You start seeing yourself as this creature, the five, six sense animal,

67
00:04:40,598 --> 00:04:43,121
[SPEAKER_01]: That senses will operate for as long as you live.

68
00:04:43,241 --> 00:04:45,343
[SPEAKER_01]: They will want to pull you in direction they want to go.

69
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:51,662
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's you to stop gratuitously identifying with them and giving in to their pressure.

70
00:04:52,503 --> 00:04:54,523
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they'll obey, eventually.

71
00:04:55,324 --> 00:04:57,425
[SPEAKER_01]: How long that will take is how much effort you put in.

72
00:04:57,725 --> 00:04:59,966
[SPEAKER_00]: And the chief is the mind.

73
00:05:00,106 --> 00:05:02,767
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the animal which really needs to be...

74
00:05:02,827 --> 00:05:03,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

75
00:05:03,708 --> 00:05:08,750
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind directs all the other animals and the mind is basically that which is the hardest to tame.

76
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:10,851
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the same principle.

77
00:05:11,731 --> 00:05:16,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize you have such and such thoughts, but then you reflect further.

78
00:05:17,472 --> 00:05:27,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, the mind... Like, when the mind is sort of interested in sensuality, for example, the mind will not think about the ugly aspects that are simultaneously there.

79
00:05:28,335 --> 00:05:29,715
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can make it do so.

80
00:05:31,256 --> 00:05:32,696
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what he decided to do there.

81
00:05:32,836 --> 00:05:36,417
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I will tie you down, this mind, and I'll stay where you told me to go.

82
00:05:37,169 --> 00:05:37,729
[SPEAKER_01]: I accept that.

83
00:05:37,809 --> 00:05:38,569
[SPEAKER_01]: That was a good idea.

84
00:05:38,630 --> 00:05:39,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

85
00:05:39,630 --> 00:05:41,170
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, we're not changing this.

86
00:05:41,591 --> 00:05:44,471
[SPEAKER_01]: We're gonna stay here until you listen to me, not other way around.

87
00:05:44,491 --> 00:05:50,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about not having those thoughts.

88
00:05:50,113 --> 00:05:51,694
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about preventing them from arising.

89
00:05:51,734 --> 00:05:52,914
[SPEAKER_01]: I shouldn't be thinking this.

90
00:05:53,555 --> 00:05:53,735
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

91
00:05:54,715 --> 00:05:56,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not up to you what you think.

92
00:05:58,156 --> 00:05:58,876
[SPEAKER_01]: It's up to your mind.

93
00:05:59,357 --> 00:06:01,077
[SPEAKER_01]: But you should not be acting out of it.

94
00:06:01,857 --> 00:06:03,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting upon it and accepting it.

95
00:06:04,476 --> 00:06:06,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Without further consideration.

96
00:06:06,658 --> 00:06:07,318
[SPEAKER_01]: That's on you.

97
00:06:08,959 --> 00:06:11,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know exactly where the line is.

98
00:06:11,441 --> 00:06:12,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is not my responsibility.

99
00:06:12,802 --> 00:06:14,303
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind can come with whatever it wants.

100
00:06:14,903 --> 00:06:17,665
[SPEAKER_01]: But I will not entertain it, encourage it, if it's unwholesome.

101
00:06:20,947 --> 00:06:22,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's up to you to choose.

102
00:06:23,269 --> 00:06:29,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's up to you to choose to accept the offer of the mind or not, to go with it, along with it, to act upon it or not.

103
00:06:31,257 --> 00:06:39,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, it's also... it's up to you to choose whether to... to allow mind to... to keep dwelling and revolve.

104
00:06:39,261 --> 00:06:44,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it needs your input, it needs your encouragement to keep revolving around, say, centrality or something.

105
00:06:45,283 --> 00:06:47,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you should develop the perception of danger in those thoughts.

106
00:06:48,045 --> 00:06:54,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while the mind is thinking those thoughts of centrality, you are not wanting them because they are dangerous.

107
00:06:54,948 --> 00:06:57,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, I will have mind never think that again.

108
00:06:59,246 --> 00:07:04,369
[SPEAKER_01]: That might be the final outcome of you being completely dispassionate towards what your mind presents you with.

109
00:07:05,369 --> 00:07:10,953
[SPEAKER_01]: But until then, the mind will be presenting you with stuff, same as any other sense will be presenting you for what it wants to do.

110
00:07:13,194 --> 00:07:20,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you must not welcome it, if you know it, if you see that it's rooted in sensuality, your will, or distraction.

111
00:07:22,682 --> 00:07:24,203
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean, I don't want it.

112
00:07:24,563 --> 00:07:26,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Let this... let make these thoughts go away.

113
00:07:26,384 --> 00:07:27,384
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it means, I don't want it.

114
00:07:27,444 --> 00:07:31,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Now I have to endure this unruly mind that is trying to harass me with these thoughts.

115
00:07:32,487 --> 00:07:46,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You were saying the other day about... there is a sutra on that... that greed is mildly blameworthy and slow to fade.

116
00:07:48,544 --> 00:07:51,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Hatred is very blameworthy, quick to fade.

117
00:07:52,687 --> 00:07:57,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Delusion is very blameworthy and slow to fade.

118
00:07:57,812 --> 00:08:01,034
[SPEAKER_00]: So, delusion is the worst.

119
00:08:01,234 --> 00:08:02,335
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the subtlest.

120
00:08:03,376 --> 00:08:08,180
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the most... well, it's the most primordial out of three, really.

121
00:08:12,241 --> 00:08:16,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So the practice, if you want to practice getting rid of delusion... Yeah.

122
00:08:17,325 --> 00:08:25,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think what the most, or certainly fairly important thing, is to make sure that it's not misunderstood.

123
00:08:26,163 --> 00:08:31,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, people, like, lust is obvious, greed is obvious, well, obvious, obviously on the level of idea.

124
00:08:31,885 --> 00:08:34,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, people, you know, when you say greed, people know, okay, that's greed.

125
00:08:35,147 --> 00:08:45,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you say delusion, I think more often than not, people would would think of it as as, like, some sort of confusion, ignorance of something you don't know.

126
00:08:45,451 --> 00:08:49,053
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're deluded, in some, almost like a metaphysical sense.

127
00:08:50,353 --> 00:08:58,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you become enlightened now, you penetrate it through the delusion and, you know, the secrets have been revealed and there is no more delusion in you.

128
00:08:59,043 --> 00:09:03,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not something, some piece of information that you don't have.

129
00:09:03,528 --> 00:09:06,331
[SPEAKER_01]: It's existential attitude that you have towards your senses.

130
00:09:07,698 --> 00:09:09,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards the sense organs, the delusion.

131
00:09:10,039 --> 00:09:14,901
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I often say delusion slash distraction, because it's an active thing.

132
00:09:15,181 --> 00:09:16,561
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, oh, yeah, delusion.

133
00:09:16,601 --> 00:09:17,262
[SPEAKER_01]: It's somewhere there.

134
00:09:17,502 --> 00:09:25,565
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm working through these methods and techniques and I'm reading the suttas and, you know, somehow I'm affecting this accumulated delusion at the back of my mind.

135
00:09:25,625 --> 00:09:25,745
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

136
00:09:26,145 --> 00:09:28,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusion is something you do here and now.

137
00:09:28,786 --> 00:09:31,587
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, greed is something you would be doing here and now.

138
00:09:32,595 --> 00:09:33,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Or be liable to it.

139
00:09:34,036 --> 00:09:36,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And anger is something you will be doing here and now.

140
00:09:36,276 --> 00:09:37,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Or be liable to it.

141
00:09:37,317 --> 00:09:38,797
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's greed, aversion, delusion.

142
00:09:39,778 --> 00:09:46,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's on the same practical basis of something that you welcome, and act upon, and accept, and don't see for what it is.

143
00:09:47,901 --> 00:09:50,042
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like something you don't know.

144
00:09:51,402 --> 00:09:54,263
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically something that you keep doing, that is mistaken.

145
00:09:55,043 --> 00:09:58,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards the senses, towards engagement with the world...

146
00:09:58,725 --> 00:09:59,365
[SPEAKER_00]: The attitude of...

147
00:10:00,011 --> 00:10:11,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the attitude of, basically, the attitude of distraction, it's about that, the movement of distraction, the movement of having something to do, for example.

148
00:10:14,141 --> 00:10:18,424
[SPEAKER_01]: A good indicator, as we spoke before, would be to measure it with boredom.

149
00:10:20,425 --> 00:10:26,730
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are easily, if somebody who is easily bored, means you have accumulated a lot of delusion, they will be hard to see and hard to fade.

150
00:10:28,027 --> 00:10:33,215
[SPEAKER_01]: If you never get bored, well, you're probably at least anāgāmī or maybe an arahant.

151
00:10:33,255 --> 00:10:35,738
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no pressure of non-activity at all.

152
00:10:37,381 --> 00:10:38,583
[SPEAKER_01]: No pressure to do anything.

153
00:10:39,284 --> 00:10:43,510
[SPEAKER_01]: No pressure to look away or distract yourself from nothing happening, nothing being felt.

154
00:10:45,070 --> 00:10:49,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically speaking, boredom is a good way to practice as well.

155
00:10:50,371 --> 00:10:54,692
[SPEAKER_01]: And often, you know, like, keeping the precepts from your life, that's what's going to result into.

156
00:10:55,152 --> 00:11:02,254
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, then you have the problem of people don't see boredom as an indicator, and they certainly don't see a delusion as an attitude.

157
00:11:02,274 --> 00:11:08,015
[SPEAKER_01]: They still cultivate, despite their commitment to precepts, and commitment to suttas, and commitment to meditation.

158
00:11:08,775 --> 00:11:11,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, they always try to deal with boredom.

159
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:20,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even when they're practicing, your meditation technique becomes something you do on account of avoiding boredom.

160
00:11:20,966 --> 00:11:25,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Your reading the suttas, studies, scholarly work, becomes something you do on account of avoiding boredom.

161
00:11:27,531 --> 00:11:29,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you need to know the suttas.

162
00:11:29,793 --> 00:11:33,095
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to collect this information in order to know what to look for.

163
00:11:33,495 --> 00:11:36,518
[SPEAKER_01]: But, sooner or later, you need to start uprooting delusion for what it is.

164
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:47,207
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you need to endure and not depend always on some activity to keep you occupied and engaged and basically connected to the senses.

165
00:11:48,128 --> 00:11:50,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, you decide to get bored.

166
00:11:50,190 --> 00:11:54,793
[SPEAKER_01]: So, switch your phone off, no interest in anything, close your doors, pull the shutters.

167
00:11:55,454 --> 00:11:58,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter whether you sit, stand, walk around your apartment.

168
00:11:58,276 --> 00:12:00,298
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not obviously watch anything, read anything.

169
00:12:00,838 --> 00:12:05,561
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not do your meditation technique and watch your nostrils and perform these methods of doing for an hour.

170
00:12:06,081 --> 00:12:08,782
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just going to abide in non-activity, as the suttas would say.

171
00:12:09,783 --> 00:12:12,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind will be presenting you, do this, do that.

172
00:12:13,084 --> 00:12:13,605
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

173
00:12:14,785 --> 00:12:16,266
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't try to get rid of those thoughts.

174
00:12:16,366 --> 00:12:18,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You just don't act on it.

175
00:12:18,507 --> 00:12:20,288
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, I'll get up.

176
00:12:20,368 --> 00:12:21,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I can get up.

177
00:12:21,128 --> 00:12:23,550
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not an activity of a kind that would distract you.

178
00:12:24,397 --> 00:12:25,138
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll go to toilet.

179
00:12:25,158 --> 00:12:27,179
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not an activity of a kind to distract you.

180
00:12:27,579 --> 00:12:30,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, let me just check my... oh, that's an activity of a kind, so I will not do that.

181
00:12:32,503 --> 00:12:34,985
[SPEAKER_01]: You probably do that for an hour, you're gonna feel intense boredom.

182
00:12:35,485 --> 00:12:50,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why people fear that solitude, confinement, when there is nothing to distract their senses and give that impending sort of prospect of doing something, completing something, finding something, seeing something, hearing something, resolving, performing something.

183
00:12:51,622 --> 00:12:52,803
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's frightening.

184
00:12:52,983 --> 00:12:53,764
[SPEAKER_01]: But in itself...

185
00:12:53,784 --> 00:12:54,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Being something.

186
00:12:54,745 --> 00:12:55,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

187
00:12:55,205 --> 00:12:56,406
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself, that's not frightening.

188
00:12:57,107 --> 00:13:01,971
[SPEAKER_01]: That's only frightening because you've been depending on the momentum of action always.

189
00:13:03,212 --> 00:13:08,577
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's gratuitous dependence that you've been cultivating because you didn't know any better.

190
00:13:09,277 --> 00:13:11,818
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you don't know any better, you will be cultivating.

191
00:13:12,218 --> 00:13:14,599
[SPEAKER_01]: But, so that's when we... Are you delighting in projects?

192
00:13:14,679 --> 00:13:16,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you delighting in work?

193
00:13:16,479 --> 00:13:18,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you are delighting in distraction.

194
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:21,221
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also, like, where your practice of samādhi can be.

195
00:13:22,001 --> 00:13:25,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Just try and abide in true non-activity for an hour or two.

196
00:13:25,922 --> 00:13:29,223
[SPEAKER_01]: No meditation techniques, no trying to be mindful, performing mindfulness.

197
00:13:29,563 --> 00:13:30,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Just sit there.

198
00:13:30,944 --> 00:13:31,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't fall asleep.

199
00:13:32,364 --> 00:13:33,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Get up if you're sleepy.

200
00:13:33,365 --> 00:13:34,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Stand or walk.

201
00:13:35,523 --> 00:13:42,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, just allow your mind to keep coming up with these ideas, what to do, planning, and then don't commit to them.

202
00:13:42,730 --> 00:13:45,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Just let them endure there and sit about.

203
00:13:46,914 --> 00:13:57,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And do that for three or four hours, and you see, basically, there's going to be space created, unwittingly for most people, initially, between you and your senses.

204
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:03,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that identification that I mentioned in the beginning, that people naturally jump into with the senses.

205
00:14:03,366 --> 00:14:04,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense wants to see, I want to see.

206
00:14:05,409 --> 00:14:07,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense wants to hear, I want to hear.

207
00:14:08,112 --> 00:14:13,380
[SPEAKER_01]: That needs maintenance of that proximity, of that closeness, of that immediate acting upon impulse.

208
00:14:14,541 --> 00:14:16,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't act upon impulse,

209
00:14:16,930 --> 00:14:17,550
[SPEAKER_01]: You're restrained.

210
00:14:17,891 --> 00:14:21,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, even your thoughts of, hey, look at this, do that, plan that.

211
00:14:21,613 --> 00:14:22,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, let that endure.

212
00:14:22,854 --> 00:14:24,114
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not, I'm not accepting that.

213
00:14:24,595 --> 00:14:25,395
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll probably do it later.

214
00:14:25,415 --> 00:14:26,516
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing unwholesome with it.

215
00:14:26,596 --> 00:14:29,097
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, I'm concerned about non-activity.

216
00:14:29,898 --> 00:14:34,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You will create that space because you're not now jumping upon every impulse of your sense.

217
00:14:35,261 --> 00:14:38,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get to actually see the creature for what it is.

218
00:14:38,484 --> 00:14:51,294
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind there, like, with thoughts, want to do this, want to do that, the monkey mind, the, you know, all the eyes, all these animal comparisons to the senses, you get to see it, because you stop being kind of driven by them, like, on a closed leash.

219
00:14:52,715 --> 00:14:53,716
[SPEAKER_01]: You actually stepped away.

220
00:14:54,997 --> 00:15:01,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, even if you choose to do something, say, if you establish now that, if that's your practice for most of the day, abiding in non-activity,

221
00:15:03,835 --> 00:15:05,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Wakeful, because you're not sleeping, obviously.

222
00:15:06,676 --> 00:15:11,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, just... just seeing where your mind comes up, it endures the next thing, the next thing.

223
00:15:12,178 --> 00:15:23,981
[SPEAKER_01]: But, when you do commit to do something with your body, with your mouth, with your speech, with your mind, you do so only on the level of that kind of recognition that this will not cause further distraction.

224
00:15:25,402 --> 00:15:28,583
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, those things that you do, you do them through the mind of non-activity.

225
00:15:30,022 --> 00:15:36,264
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why in jhāna, you can, you know, walk, eat, go to toilet, you know, patch your robe, do stuff.

226
00:15:37,105 --> 00:15:39,966
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you're frozen, motionless, because it's non-activity.

227
00:15:40,026 --> 00:15:40,726
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

228
00:15:40,826 --> 00:15:51,750
[SPEAKER_01]: It's meant by non-activity means non... non... no activity that's rooted in distraction, that it's motivated by that fundamental delusion to distract yourself.

229
00:15:54,723 --> 00:15:56,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's that's the measure there.

230
00:15:56,405 --> 00:15:59,429
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it will also become clear what you are not distracting yourself from.

231
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:00,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Yourself.

232
00:16:01,692 --> 00:16:03,013
[SPEAKER_01]: These creatures of the senses.

233
00:16:03,113 --> 00:16:03,594
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure.

234
00:16:04,435 --> 00:16:05,877
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people fear boredom.

235
00:16:06,598 --> 00:16:07,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm bored.

236
00:16:07,799 --> 00:16:08,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Stay bored a bit longer.

237
00:16:09,041 --> 00:16:10,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm afraid now.

238
00:16:10,843 --> 00:16:13,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, boredom will always turn into anxiety.

239
00:16:14,721 --> 00:16:16,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you start seeing your existence.

240
00:16:16,282 --> 00:16:24,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you start seeing how basically divorced from your own senses you are, from things that you identify with, that defines what you are.

241
00:16:25,445 --> 00:16:35,129
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like, as I said, it's like, you know, like somebody releases a helium balloon that just starts, you know, untethered, just goes higher and higher and higher and higher.

242
00:16:35,729 --> 00:16:38,630
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your mind now, because there is no end to how high it can go.

243
00:16:39,858 --> 00:16:41,499
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no problem in how high it goes.

244
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:47,704
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is from your expectation and need and dependence of being tethered to the senses.

245
00:16:48,585 --> 00:16:52,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't been restraining yourself enough, you haven't been seeing the danger in the slightest fall.

246
00:16:52,909 --> 00:16:56,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have been depending on action of your senses.

247
00:16:58,032 --> 00:17:06,234
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's why, like, you have many, you know, you met in your life, everybody, have met people in life, who have, who always depend on having something to do.

248
00:17:06,414 --> 00:17:09,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody depends on having something to do, but some people more than others.

249
00:17:10,215 --> 00:17:22,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when they constantly need to be active, if they're not active, haha, I'm bored, and then restlessness set in, and then anxiety, and everything, doesn't matter how insignificant, becomes the most important thing in the world that must be done.

250
00:17:23,097 --> 00:17:34,413
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you're dying, you're barely moving, you're 90 years old, but your whole life has been depending on having something to do, and you will still be trying to do stuff, because otherwise it's just too frightening, too overwhelming.

251
00:17:35,687 --> 00:17:48,190
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that is only because you refuse to give up the gratuitous assumptions that these senses are yours, are worth holding on to, are the basis for identification.

252
00:17:48,511 --> 00:18:00,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you give that up, then the loftiness of non-activity, the expanse, the immeasurability of the non-active mind, mind withdrawn from dependence on the sense activity, is the most sublime thing.

253
00:18:03,486 --> 00:18:13,511
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as we described before, the pleasure of jhāna, the pleasure of the neutral feeling, needs to be understood, needs to be learned, needs to be developed.

254
00:18:13,931 --> 00:18:14,832
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not automatic.

255
00:18:18,094 --> 00:18:30,820
[SPEAKER_01]: And, by the way, that's why, like, say, you haven't had anything to do for five hours, you were confined in a room, and then now you're released, and it doesn't matter.

256
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:31,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the most

257
00:18:33,038 --> 00:18:33,998
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the dirtiest of the chores.

258
00:18:34,018 --> 00:18:35,199
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to go and clean the toilet.

259
00:18:35,299 --> 00:18:41,481
[SPEAKER_01]: You will enjoy it, because at least, at least, there's something for you to do, if you're still dependent on the action.

260
00:18:42,822 --> 00:18:50,344
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize now, the pleasure of having something to do is determined from how far your mind has went away, how far has it withdrawn.

261
00:18:51,345 --> 00:18:57,147
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you see, people who never experience boredom, because they always have something lined up to do and distract themselves with,

262
00:18:58,057 --> 00:19:01,363
[SPEAKER_01]: There are people who then double down even more onto sensuality.

263
00:19:01,643 --> 00:19:04,488
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing, by the way, there's nothing sensual with cleaning the toilet.

264
00:19:05,249 --> 00:19:05,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And it isn't.

265
00:19:06,551 --> 00:19:11,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So you just enjoy the action in itself, because the boredom was a bit too much to handle.

266
00:19:12,327 --> 00:19:19,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, you never really experience boredom, you never experience any separation from the senses, like stepping back, no perspective in regard to it.

267
00:19:20,508 --> 00:19:26,449
[SPEAKER_01]: All you do is engage with the senses, that's when you now need even more engagement for any sense of pleasure.

268
00:19:26,489 --> 00:19:32,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the more you engage with sensuality, the less pleasure you feel, the more you need to engage.

269
00:19:33,571 --> 00:19:39,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, you've been withdrawn from sensuality for a long time, even a little bit of engagement with the sense will be very agreeable and pleasant.

270
00:19:40,292 --> 00:19:41,992
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't constitute sensuality.

271
00:19:43,082 --> 00:19:45,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Engagement with the sense in itself doesn't constitute sensuality.

272
00:19:47,223 --> 00:20:02,909
[SPEAKER_01]: The further now you have distracted yourself, you use the engagement with the senses basically for the basis of your own self-delusion from yourself, now you seek the further pleasure of the sense objects, that's where your sensuality sets in.

273
00:20:02,989 --> 00:20:12,112
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the Buddha described the difference between pleasure that is underlied by more craving, by lust for more pleasure, and pleasure that isn't.

274
00:20:12,998 --> 00:20:17,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, pleasure of cleaning a toilet is not underlined for more pleasure to do it.

275
00:20:18,482 --> 00:20:21,844
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually determined by the fact that you haven't been engaging with sensuality.

276
00:20:22,884 --> 00:20:24,245
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't need to fear that pleasure.

277
00:20:25,106 --> 00:20:33,952
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you need to be careful though, because you will then be trying to find out random things to do, because they are not sensual, but they can become distracting.

278
00:20:34,552 --> 00:20:37,434
[SPEAKER_01]: And if they do, then your mind will start thinking sensual thoughts.

279
00:20:38,148 --> 00:20:38,968
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes in that direction.

280
00:20:38,988 --> 00:20:39,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

281
00:20:39,629 --> 00:20:40,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Goes further in that direction.

282
00:20:41,009 --> 00:20:44,531
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the same principle, taken even further.

283
00:20:44,711 --> 00:20:46,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, say you don't fear the boredom anymore.

284
00:20:46,732 --> 00:20:48,253
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't fear the neutral feeling.

285
00:20:48,353 --> 00:20:50,394
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not felt unpleasantly, because you understood it.

286
00:20:51,115 --> 00:20:53,036
[SPEAKER_01]: You basically live withdrawn from sensuality.

287
00:20:53,096 --> 00:20:54,436
[SPEAKER_01]: You have developed the jhāna.

288
00:20:55,157 --> 00:21:01,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your mind is now even more established upon the greater separation from the senses and sense pools and so on.

289
00:21:02,557 --> 00:21:08,868
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why then, from that perspective, even enduring discomfort will be felt like divine pleasure.

290
00:21:10,019 --> 00:21:16,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from the point of view of boredom, cleaning the toilet will be felt extremely exhilarating.

291
00:21:17,421 --> 00:21:32,304
[SPEAKER_01]: From the point of view of jhāna, physical endurance of torture, rough robes, bare food and medicine, living under the root of a tree, will be felt absolutely like the finest robes, the finest food, the finest medicine.

292
00:21:32,344 --> 00:21:33,185
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same principle.

293
00:21:33,665 --> 00:21:39,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Determined by how much your mind is withdrawn, that determines how pleasant the engagement will be.

294
00:21:41,075 --> 00:21:42,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in itself.

295
00:21:42,676 --> 00:21:47,398
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why for some people, like the whole world of sensuality, whatever they want, they feel numb.

296
00:21:47,719 --> 00:21:50,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they've been engaging with that level of senses for so long.

297
00:21:50,680 --> 00:21:51,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is not withdrawn.

298
00:21:52,481 --> 00:21:54,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same principle of withdrawal.

299
00:21:55,700 --> 00:22:07,630
[SPEAKER_01]: In boredom, if you work through the boredom, don't act out of it, endure it, even endure that anxiety if it arises, then even chores will be seen as agreeable, as fine.

300
00:22:08,691 --> 00:22:15,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will still not lose, unless you choose to, you will not lose that recognition that, oh, the non-activity,

301
00:22:16,798 --> 00:22:20,601
[SPEAKER_01]: State of mind that has arisen on account of enduring boredom and not acting upon the sense.

302
00:22:21,462 --> 00:22:25,706
[SPEAKER_01]: See, because of that, I have pleasant abiding here and now, when I do have to engage with the senses.

303
00:22:27,467 --> 00:22:29,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you develop that further into a jhāna.

304
00:22:30,810 --> 00:22:39,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, even if you don't do any chores, just enduring the elements will be agreeable, will be seen pleasantly, from the point of view of mind so withdrawn.

305
00:22:41,296 --> 00:22:43,499
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I would encourage Venerable Anuruddin.

306
00:22:43,519 --> 00:22:44,220
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said.

307
00:22:44,260 --> 00:22:48,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice jhāna, so that the rough robes you wear will feel like silk.

308
00:22:49,948 --> 00:22:53,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of the mind of jhāna, mind of withdrawal from the senses.

309
00:22:54,418 --> 00:22:54,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

310
00:22:54,978 --> 00:22:55,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to fear.

311
00:22:56,159 --> 00:23:00,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but, so, me enjoying these rough robes, oh, that's sensuality.

312
00:23:00,300 --> 00:23:05,982
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... Only a person who is not free from sensuality would use that as a ref... and think that everything is sensuality.

313
00:23:06,762 --> 00:23:08,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the only reference you have.

314
00:23:09,223 --> 00:23:12,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the Buddha himself initially thought that pleasure of that withdrawal

315
00:23:14,325 --> 00:23:25,120
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unwholesome, but then says, wait, why do I fear this pleasure that is not connected with that double down pleasure of sense objects, that you have to be craving for more if you engage with?

316
00:23:25,260 --> 00:23:26,923
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always underlied by more.

317
00:23:28,357 --> 00:23:29,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is not underlined.

318
00:23:29,358 --> 00:23:30,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do I fear it?

319
00:23:30,198 --> 00:23:31,239
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I don't fear it?

320
00:23:31,699 --> 00:23:33,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he realized it shouldn't be feared.

321
00:23:34,121 --> 00:23:44,408
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're enjoying the living in the in the in the jungle, wearing the rough robes and enduring insects, if you find that's agreeable, you don't need to have that centrality, because it isn't.

322
00:23:45,617 --> 00:23:49,958
[SPEAKER_01]: That agreeability of it is determined by your state of loftiness of your mind.

323
00:23:50,558 --> 00:23:51,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not distracting yourself.

324
00:23:52,238 --> 00:23:54,178
[SPEAKER_01]: You will inevitably find things enjoyable.

325
00:23:54,519 --> 00:24:02,620
[SPEAKER_01]: If you develop jhānas, as they did in the suttas, even like the extreme physical rotting guts and stuff, like, no, it's alright, I'll endure this, no problem.

326
00:24:03,140 --> 00:24:05,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Because my mind can obtain jhānas at will.

327
00:24:05,520 --> 00:24:11,661
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, my mind can re-establish itself as fully withdrawn and independent of any sense at will.

328
00:24:12,141 --> 00:24:14,462
[SPEAKER_01]: How can I be bothered by anything that comes through any sense?

329
00:24:15,425 --> 00:24:24,810
[SPEAKER_01]: When Mahākassapa was offered by the Buddha, because he was already old, to leave the rough forest, stop living, keeping all those dūtanga, ascetic practices, because it's it's too much.

330
00:24:24,870 --> 00:24:26,571
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, you did your work.

331
00:24:26,611 --> 00:24:27,731
[SPEAKER_01]: There's really no need for this.

332
00:24:27,811 --> 00:24:28,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Come on.

333
00:24:28,491 --> 00:24:30,833
[SPEAKER_01]: I get plenty of robes, plenty of food, plenty of lodging.

334
00:24:30,853 --> 00:24:32,013
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll pass it down onto you.

335
00:24:33,274 --> 00:24:35,595
[SPEAKER_01]: There's really no need for you to torture yourself.

336
00:24:35,975 --> 00:24:37,916
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, oh, no, Lord, this is not a torture.

337
00:24:38,336 --> 00:24:42,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Whoever can obtain jhānas at will, this is what they want to do.

338
00:24:42,398 --> 00:24:43,639
[SPEAKER_01]: This is where their joy is.

339
00:24:46,295 --> 00:24:49,737
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they will say, oh, you're truly practicing for the sake of future generation.

340
00:24:49,877 --> 00:24:51,719
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, that's the inspiring thing to do.

341
00:24:52,479 --> 00:24:53,180
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to do it.

342
00:24:53,220 --> 00:24:54,921
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no further dhamma to be developed.

343
00:24:54,941 --> 00:24:55,901
[SPEAKER_01]: You completed everything.

344
00:24:56,342 --> 00:25:01,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you do it for the sake of pleasure and joy that you find in it, because you can obtain jhānas at will.

345
00:25:03,160 --> 00:25:07,403
[SPEAKER_01]: And he, you know, there were no records of him how he died, but he must have died in the same environment.

346
00:25:08,344 --> 00:25:11,946
[SPEAKER_01]: He never left the hardship and ascetic practices, because they were not there.

347
00:25:12,547 --> 00:25:17,550
[SPEAKER_01]: The relativity of hardship and endurance is determined by how much your mind is withdrawn from these things or not.

348
00:25:19,333 --> 00:25:24,437
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is... that's why it's not incidental that boredom is always that fundamental thing that nobody wants to deal with.

349
00:25:24,457 --> 00:25:31,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Even people that practice the dhamma, the practice of dhamma, because something you do to avoid that sinking into that boredom.

350
00:25:32,722 --> 00:25:40,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, say you've been sitting for three hours, closed in your room, everything switched off, no notifications, no activity, and you want to get up and drink tea.

351
00:25:40,148 --> 00:25:40,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

352
00:25:41,068 --> 00:25:44,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Now your mind starts... wants to overthink it, wants to get into details.

353
00:25:44,931 --> 00:25:46,032
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you don't welcome.

354
00:25:46,152 --> 00:25:47,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean I will never drink tea again.

355
00:25:48,873 --> 00:25:50,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you see the mind.

356
00:25:50,495 --> 00:25:50,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

357
00:25:50,815 --> 00:26:04,227
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not lose the sight of yourself, which means even making of the tea will not become a basis for that self-deluding, whereby you become unaware of yourself and you fall into the identification of the senses you're engaging with.

358
00:26:04,287 --> 00:26:07,950
[SPEAKER_01]: You still maintain that distance, the right perspective.

359
00:26:08,322 --> 00:26:17,806
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are living in such a way, non-distraction, doing things not to distract yourself, your life becomes simple.

360
00:26:19,347 --> 00:26:20,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Naturally, sure.

361
00:26:20,227 --> 00:26:22,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Naturally results in simplicity.

362
00:26:23,309 --> 00:26:29,171
[SPEAKER_01]: The amount of proliferation and elaboration towards the senses and engagement with it, yeah, that will have to diminish.

363
00:26:29,611 --> 00:26:30,192
[SPEAKER_01]: That would fail.

364
00:26:31,492 --> 00:26:35,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why working, talking and sleeping, not sensual in itself,

365
00:26:39,399 --> 00:26:52,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the one with the right view, who, if he were to practice sense restraint and learn to abide in non-activity even more, will become an arahant in this life.

366
00:26:53,446 --> 00:26:58,089
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, they get distracted with work, talk and sleep.

367
00:26:58,829 --> 00:27:01,691
[SPEAKER_01]: That becomes basically obstruction to their own progress.

368
00:27:02,599 --> 00:27:06,382
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha would tell off Ananda and other, you know, Aryas.

369
00:27:07,262 --> 00:27:09,163
[SPEAKER_01]: They were, like, busy sewing robes and stuff.

370
00:27:09,844 --> 00:27:12,686
[SPEAKER_01]: It was obviously not necessary to the extent that they were doing.

371
00:27:13,907 --> 00:27:16,508
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha really rebuked them.

372
00:27:18,410 --> 00:27:19,130
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not sensual.

373
00:27:19,686 --> 00:27:21,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Enjoying work is not sensual.

374
00:27:21,467 --> 00:27:22,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Enjoying talk is not sensual.

375
00:27:23,048 --> 00:27:24,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless, obviously, the talk is about sensuality.

376
00:27:25,310 --> 00:27:48,165
[SPEAKER_01]: If you lose the context, and if you don't maintain that transparency in regard to the motivation behind engagement with work, talk and sleep, it will become a basis for maintaining... Obviously, for, you know, for an ārya, he maintains that delusion much subtler than a puṭṭhujana would be doing, but it's still a form of that self-destruction.

377
00:27:49,285 --> 00:28:03,553
[SPEAKER_01]: And all he would need to do is not engage, or not allow himself to be engaged with work for the purpose of distraction, talk for the purpose of distraction, and sleep for the purpose of distraction, and he will progress towards nibbāna.

378
00:28:04,413 --> 00:28:05,714
[SPEAKER_01]: No other effort he needs to make.

379
00:28:06,895 --> 00:28:07,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he already understood.

380
00:28:08,275 --> 00:28:09,656
[SPEAKER_00]: The effort of non-activity.

381
00:28:09,876 --> 00:28:10,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

382
00:28:10,376 --> 00:28:12,758
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the effort of protecting the mind of non-activity.

383
00:28:13,508 --> 00:28:14,808
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all in the suttas what you do.

384
00:28:14,948 --> 00:28:16,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Abide in non-activity.

385
00:28:16,369 --> 00:28:21,590
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a pleasant abiding here and now, paired with your right view, is basically jhāna that leads you to nibbāna.

386
00:28:23,531 --> 00:28:27,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said many times, jhāna is not a product of your activity.

387
00:28:29,512 --> 00:28:33,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Jhāna is the product of withdrawal from the senses, pleasure and security that comes from it.

388
00:28:33,994 --> 00:28:35,494
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the true non-activity.

389
00:28:37,291 --> 00:28:42,773
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, non-activity is basically whether you are motivated by distraction or not.

390
00:28:42,853 --> 00:28:45,053
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not whether you are motionless or not.

391
00:28:45,633 --> 00:28:51,915
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might just jump into the conclusion, ah, non-activity means motionless for three hours, I will refuse to move because there will be a distraction of activity.

392
00:28:51,975 --> 00:28:52,175
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

393
00:28:54,796 --> 00:28:57,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-activity means non-distraction.

394
00:28:57,517 --> 00:28:59,277
[SPEAKER_01]: So, are you abiding in non-distraction?

395
00:28:59,337 --> 00:29:00,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that, by the way.

396
00:29:00,698 --> 00:29:02,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, as I said, just sit in a flat,

397
00:29:03,667 --> 00:29:08,910
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, within five minutes, the mind will present you something to do, and you will catch yourself.

398
00:29:09,710 --> 00:29:15,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you relishing the idea of, yes, let me do this, let me click that button, let me open that door, let me look at this.

399
00:29:16,835 --> 00:29:17,795
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, there you go.

400
00:29:18,475 --> 00:29:19,596
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the activity.

401
00:29:19,956 --> 00:29:20,997
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the distraction.

402
00:29:22,275 --> 00:29:27,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Not acting upon an impulse to act, it's already creating some space between you and your senses.

403
00:29:27,738 --> 00:29:35,583
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why none of this would be possible unless you keep the precepts beforehand and practice and restrain your senses.

404
00:29:35,603 --> 00:29:45,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-activities, not being motivated and not using any type of action for the means of distraction of yourself from yourself.

405
00:29:46,947 --> 00:29:57,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not use any type of action for the basis for identification with the senses, for losing the perspective of the creature that needs to be contained and restrained and kept an eye on.

406
00:29:59,439 --> 00:30:03,063
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's nothing like the decrease of delusion and the right perspective.

407
00:30:03,623 --> 00:30:05,205
[SPEAKER_00]: They're keeping that perspective.

408
00:30:06,937 --> 00:30:08,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, sure, sure, sure.

409
00:30:08,478 --> 00:30:09,939
[SPEAKER_01]: The increase of that...

410
00:30:10,119 --> 00:30:16,584
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start giving in to these distracting activities, the perspective, the right perspective, will diminish or will be more obscured.

411
00:30:18,345 --> 00:30:22,608
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't give in to these acts of self-distraction, the perspective is maintained.

412
00:30:22,888 --> 00:30:23,609
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to...

413
00:30:23,889 --> 00:30:24,930
[SPEAKER_01]: I must keep this perspective.

414
00:30:24,950 --> 00:30:27,992
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is protect it by not distracting myself from it.

415
00:30:28,012 --> 00:30:29,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's that indirect...

416
00:30:29,611 --> 00:30:51,454
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you stop being engaged with the world, like with work or whatever else you have to do, now you realize, oh, there is enough space for me here to see if the actions I'm about to do and what I'm doing when I'm not working, do I need to, and to what extent I'm using that for basically pleasure of... of kind of indolence and forgetting about this creature that I do need to keep an eye on.

417
00:30:53,550 --> 00:30:59,754
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need, as I said before, you don't need to be choking creature and constantly tying it up, but you do need to not lose the sight of it.

418
00:31:00,835 --> 00:31:04,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now that you finished your work, when you come back at home, you need to rest.

419
00:31:04,637 --> 00:31:12,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that include binge-watching, I don't know, a four-hour long movie, having a drink, listening to the music?

420
00:31:13,363 --> 00:31:16,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Where does it... where does it say that you need to do that for relaxation?

421
00:31:17,826 --> 00:31:20,248
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do for relaxation is not do any work.

422
00:31:22,281 --> 00:31:34,688
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, okay, yeah, so I can easily relax myself and enjoy doing nothing, or enjoy doing something that's non-distracting, and that will not result in me wanting to do more of it and losing the sight of myself.

423
00:31:35,189 --> 00:31:36,349
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I could then...

424
00:31:37,550 --> 00:31:40,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, choose to do things that would result in such things.

425
00:31:40,231 --> 00:31:46,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, watching entertainment, engagement with chit-chat, people, company.

426
00:31:46,533 --> 00:31:48,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that will take you away even further from the mind.

427
00:31:48,693 --> 00:31:52,895
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although it's not technically still central, it's already heading in that direction.

428
00:31:53,295 --> 00:32:00,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Direction of depending on activity and work and so on, which means it's just a question of time when you're going to be seeking the central objects and so on.

429
00:32:00,897 --> 00:32:01,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

430
00:32:01,758 --> 00:32:07,881
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to relax, but not to the point of losing the sight of the creature you need to keep an eye on, that you are.

431
00:32:09,102 --> 00:32:10,623
[SPEAKER_01]: And often people conflate the two.

432
00:32:11,363 --> 00:32:15,445
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done my work, now I can relax, means I can lose the sight.

433
00:32:15,465 --> 00:32:15,566
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

434
00:32:15,846 --> 00:32:23,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you can relax, but at no point that means you are allowed to lose the sight of the creature of the six senses.

435
00:32:24,130 --> 00:32:25,091
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's a no-no.

436
00:32:26,771 --> 00:32:29,373
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's that difference between activity and non-activity.

437
00:32:29,853 --> 00:32:33,875
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't lose the sight of the creature, you will not be active on the level of the creature.

438
00:32:34,036 --> 00:32:37,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're doing the chores, you will be doing them through the mind of non-activity.

439
00:32:37,858 --> 00:32:45,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Only to the minimum point of complete the need, the purpose, and then you're back into non-activity and abiding in jhāna, basically.

440
00:32:45,783 --> 00:32:47,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with like eating, if you were to eat...

441
00:32:47,424 --> 00:32:48,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with eating, exactly.

442
00:32:48,505 --> 00:32:53,768
[SPEAKER_01]: To the extent that needs to be eaten, then finished, then not seen as even pleasurable to do more.

443
00:32:54,716 --> 00:32:59,939
[SPEAKER_01]: What is pleasurable is protecting the mind that makes everything else pleasurable, from that high stance.

444
00:33:00,659 --> 00:33:08,863
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, oh, that's the true pleasure, that's the pleasure that's not underlined by craving for more, and that pleasure is not unwholesome, it's safe, and you're mindful in it.

445
00:33:09,063 --> 00:33:09,904
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a win-win.

